d":"shake-flask","aqueous_phase":"water","doi":"10.1002/ejic.202300758","year":2024,"counterion":"F[P-](F)(F)(F)(F)F.F[P-](F)(F)(F)(F)F.F[P-](F)(F)(F)(F)F","record_id":"MLDB-000212","detection":"UV-Vis","method_description":"The partition coefficient was determined using the shake-flask method, following established protocols.[12] In essence, the complex was solubilized in ultrapure water, and its starting concentration, approximately 30 μmol L-1, was spectrophotometrically measured. This aqueous solution was then combined with an equivalent volume of noctanol, which had been pre-saturated with PBS for 24 hours. The mixture was subjected to 1 hour of automated shaking and then left undisturbed for an additional hour. Subsequent aliquots extracted from the water phase underwent electronic spectroscopy analysis to determine the complex's concentration in the aqueous medium. The n-octanol layer's concentration was deduced by contrasting it with the concentration observed in the water phase. It was then articulated as log P, where: log P = log ([Ru]octanol/[Ru]water). Each measurement was performed in triplicate."}]}